{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779313437,"nonce":"nxqdxaCntxbNMW1nTxdm++zGZig=","title":"Studio: expose task-aware delayed-reach data ports","message":"Expose delayed-reach task data as named model-visible variables instead of one vague Inputs port.\n\nContext:\n- DelayedReachTaskInputs already separates effector target, hold/go cue semantics, and target-on cue in the runtime task protocol.\n- The Studio task binding surface currently exposes a generic bindable inputs task-data item, which hides the structure that users need when wiring model inputs.\n- This work should build on bd5aefb and the task-as-scenario-boundary direction in bda4721.\n\nDecisions:\n- Model-bindable task data should come from TaskTrialSpec.inputs, not protocol-owned loss targets under TaskTrialSpec.targets.\n- Task data should expose named bindable variables such as target position, target-on cue, and hold/go cue where supported by the task schema.\n- Protocol targets/inits/interventions can remain visible in Task/Objectives surfaces but must not be draggable model inputs unless explicitly exposed as model_input data.\n- If go cue is represented as the complement of hold, the schema/runtime lowering must make that explicit rather than silently relabeling hold.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- Delayed reach Studio task data no longer presents only a generic Inputs bindable port.\n- Named task data variables have stable IDs, labels, roles, paths, dtype/shape metadata, and bindable flags.\n- Existing validation treats these named variables as task-data bindings.\n- Runtime/schema code has a clear path for extracting these values from task trial specs, even if full remote runner lowering remains constrained.\n- Tests cover named task data projection and non-bindable protocol targets.","files":null}]}